  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nPrecip Chances/Trends: The stretch of dry weather continues into \nFriday night. A potent shortwave trough and low pressure system\nwill track into northern MN then southern Canada tonight, bringing \na few lower clouds (north) and mid and high clouds (area-wide), \nbut the atmosphere looks too dry to get any rain from it. Then, as\nanother shortwave drops into the northern Plains and a frontal \nboundary approaches this weekend, chances for rain will slowly \nincrease from west to east Saturday and Saturday night, with rain \nlikely (80-100%) Saturday night into Sunday morning. Dry air looks\nto keep east-central WI dry through the evening hours, with \nlocations near Lake MI not seeing rain until after midnight, but \neventually the dry air will give way to the slug of moisture \n(PWATs just over an inch) advancing east. Probability for at least\n0.50\" of rain remains around 40-70%, highest across central and \nnorth- central WI, where this rain would help in the worsening \ndrought conditions. Chances of an inch or more are up to ~40% in \nVilas Co. Very limited instability with this system, with thunder\nchances staying under 10%.\n\nCold air wrapping into the northern part of the system will\neventually change the rain over to snow Sunday, with lake \neffect/enhanced snow shower activity lingering into Sunday evening\nas the system departs. Moisture will be limited by this point, so\nsnowfall amounts will be on the light side. Probabilities for \n0.1\" of snow have increased to 40-90% in far north-central WI \n(highest in Vilas Co.) with probabilities of more than an inch\nbetween 10-30% in the favored lake effect snow region of \nnorthwest Vilas Co. So at least a little snow is likely on the \nback side of this system, but overall impacts look to be minor.\n\nWind & Fog Trends: Southwest winds will gust to ~35 mph through\nearly afternoon, then start to diminish as mixing and winds aloft\nstart to decrease. Winds will drop off and become light and\nvariable late this evening into early Friday. This, along with\nmostly clear to partly cloudy skies, could allow for some ground \nfog to form, especially over northern WI, but dry air will limit \nthis potential. Southeast to south winds will pick up on Friday \nbut only gust to 20 kts Friday afternoon. \n\nTemperatures: Well above normal temps will prevail into Saturday,\nwith highs in the 50s to mid 60s (normal highs this time of year\nare in the upper 30s to lower 40s). Record highs that are in the \nupper 50s and low 60s Friday and Saturday look to be in jeopardy,\nespecially Green Bay, Wausau, Manitowoc and Sturgeon Bay. Several\nrecord high minimums look to be set Saturday as well, with Friday\nnight lows only dropping into upper 40s to low 50s, provided it \ndoes not cool off too fast before midnight. Much cooler air \narrives behind the low pressure system next week with highs \ntumbling in the 30s to around 40 and lows in the teens and 20s and\nwind chills in the teens and 20s.",
